"Defining and Calling Functions"
  - Function calls do not have to be variables. average(x,y) could simply be average(8,10)
  - Printf statements can call functions instead of using a direct variable
    - Used this way, functions are not saved anywhere. They are used once, then thrown away

"Function Definitions"
  - Functions may not return arrays (only restriction on type!)
  - In C89, function with no type is presumed to be int; in C99 it's illegal to omit
  - Some programmers put the return type on a line above the function (like "unsigned long int" and other such long names)
  - Even when all parameters are the same type, they must all be marked!

"Function Calls"
  - printf() is actually a function, whose return value is ordinarily discarded. It actually returns the number of characters in the statement
      count = printf("test");
	  printf("%d", count); // Count will be 4, as test has four characters
  - You can make it abundantly clear that you're discarding the return value by using (void), an explicit typecase
      (void) printf("test\n");
  - Reaching a return statement causes the code to return to where the function was called
  - Variables in functions are normally considered local, and can be re-used in different functions as they exist in separate memory space
  - Default return type for a function is "int", if not otherwise specified. 

"Function Declarations"
  - C does not require functions be before main. They can be after, as well!
  - "Implicit Declaration": Most compilers provide a default type (int) for functions they encounter before they've been read by the code. This means errors can occur 
      when the type is not int as expected, since the function can't always go before main
  - "Explicit Function Declaration": to give your compiler a "sneak preview" of functions, you can declare them in the code without their body, simply to share the return type:
      double average(double a, double b);
      int main(void) {}
      double average (double a, double b) {return (a+b) / 2;	
  - the above is known as a 'function prototype', to differentiate it from an older style of function declaration in which parenthesis are left empty.
  - Prototype must provide complete description of how to call a function, how many arguments to supply, what their types should be, and what type is returned
  - It is not necessary to specify the names of the functions parameters, so long as their *types* are present: double average (double, double);
  - Starting "C99: you *must* declare or define a function before it being used!"

"Arguments"
  - Parameter vs Argument: Parameters are dummy names representing values to be supplied when a function is called. Arguments are expressions that appear in function calls
  - Arguments are passed by value, meaning they are not altered by the function itself (unless the function actually returns something that alters that value)
  - Arguments exist in the primary code, and Parameters are the values used in the function itself (or its prototype)
      example_function(arg1, arg2);
      int example_function(parameter1, parameter2) {}

"Argument Conversions"
  - If there is a prototype, arguments are implicitly converted to the type of the parameters in the prototype.
  - If there is no prototype, default argument promotion will occur (float -> double; char -> int; short -> int...C99 only: int -> long int, etc etc.)
  - Default argument promotion is very dangerous; if you pass a double to a function that expects an int, double is not converted down to int. In other words, undefined behavior occurs!

"Array Arguments"
  - with 1D-arrays, length is usually not specified. the function call will often give a limit instead, as a second argument:
      int sum_array(int a[], int n) {
        for (i=0; i< n; i++)
   	      sum += a[i]; 

"Compound Literals"
  - "C99 Only": Functions that require an array which is only used as an argument for that function, do not need to be named, and can instead be directly initialized and used:
    total = sum_array((int []){3, 0, 3, 4, 1}, 5);
  - You can also use variables!

"9.5: The Return Statement"
  - Common to use conditional operator (if?then:else;) in return statements to generate specific error codes as needed:
      return n >= 0 ? n : 0
  - Variables of types other than the type of the function will be converted, ex. float -> int.
  - "void" type functions can have "return;", but cannot return anything else. (Technically unnecessary, as functions return after last statement is executed)
  - If a non-void function does not use a return statement, but tries to use a value, the behavior is undefined...so "always use a return statement for non-void functions!"

"The Exit Function"
  - Almost identical to return; however, "exit causes program to immediately terminate, no matter what function it is in. return only terminates main"
  - part of <stdlib.h>, usually used like: exit(0); or exit(EXIT_SUCCESS); (EXIT_SUCCESS and EXIT_FAILURE are macros for 0 and 1, respectively)!

"9.6: Recursion"
  - Recursion in C is legal, but not used vey often. A function can call itself repeatedly, then break when certain criteria are met.

"The Quicksort Algorithm"
  - Recursion is used in sophisticated algorithms that call themselves many times
  - "divide-and-conquer": technique where large problems are split into smaller pieces that are then tackled by the same algorithm. ex. quicksort

