"11.2 - The Address and Indirection Operators"

"Indirection Operator"
  - Never apply indirection operator (*) to an uninitialized pointer. It produces undefined behavior and may even crash the program!
  - Assigning values to *p is very dangerous, as it directly changes the data stored at that address

"11.4 - Pointers as Arguments"
  - Assigning &x to a function allows the function to directly alter the memory address of the variable, instead of spawning a new address and copy (which are destroyed at end of function)

"Scanf"
  - Passing a pointer to scanf consists of using it without its Indirection operator:
      scanf("%d", p);
    This is because scanf is looking for the memory address of a variable to write to, which p will give. *p would write to the p variable itself, instead!

"Printing address of a variable"
  - printf("%p");

